<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 206 BCE – 220 CE (the Western and Eastern Han)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of Han China</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Emperor to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han.jpg" alt="Emperor Wu of Han"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a><span class="soc-credit">Unknown author · Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> ranked its people by the state’s measure. At the top stood the emperor and his kin, the kings and marquesses; below them the officials, chosen by recommendation and, from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>, schooled in the Confucian classics. Every free man could also hold one of twenty ranks of honour, granted by the emperor, which brought privileges at law and in the village. In the usual Confucian scheme of four occupations, scholars came first, then farmers, artisans and, last, merchants. This was an ideal rather than a legal code, though some laws did single out merchants, who were barred from office and from wearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> yet could grow richer than nobles. Most people were small farmers who paid taxes, and their men gave a month’s labour each year and served a term as soldiers. Slaves, perhaps one person in a hundred, stood at the bottom.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 206 BCE – 220 CE (the Western and Eastern Han)</span><span><b>People</b> 57.7 million</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 8</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The emperor</span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:35%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Kings and marquesses</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:46%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Officials and scholars</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:57%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Great landed families</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:67%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-most" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:99%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Farmers</span><span class="soc-bar-s">Most people (the rest)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:78%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-unk" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Artisans</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:89%;--k:6"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-unk" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Merchants</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:7"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Enslaved people</span><span class="soc-bar-s">0.5–1.5%</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people; ranks no one has counted are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/liu-bang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">The emperor <span class="soc-term">huangdi, tianzi</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">The Han emperor was called the Son of Heaven, and people were not allowed to say his name.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The emperor (huangdi), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/son-of-heaven">Son of Heaven</a> (tianzi), was always a man of the Liu family descended from the founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>. In theory there were no limits to his power: he was supreme judge and commander-in-chief, he alone could make or repeal laws, and he alone appointed every official paid 600 bushels or more. Subjects could not speak his name and addressed him indirectly as bixia, “beneath the steps” of the throne, and anyone who entered the palace without permission could be put to death. In practice he governed with his ministers, whose court conferences an emperor overruled at his peril. Many emperors came to the throne as children and were ruled by regents, often the empress dowager, the emperor’s mother, who could issue edicts and, if he died without an heir, choose the next emperor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a>, a regent from such a consort family, took the throne himself in 9 CE; the Han was restored in 25.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The throne passed to the son the emperor named heir apparent or, failing one, to a prince the empress dowager chose. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> himself had been a commoner who rose in the revolts against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, with the imperial family: the empress dowager, the empress, the consorts and the emperor’s children.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-han">Emperor Wen of Han</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Emperor Guangwu of Han</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/son-of-heaven">Son of Heaven</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/han-xin" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/han-xin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Kings and marquesses <span class="soc-term">zhuhou wang, liehou</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Han kings were usually relatives of the emperor, and they lived on taxes from the lands they were given.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Below the emperor stood the kings, his brothers, uncles, nephews and sons other than the heir. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> first gave kingdoms to his generals, but by 196 BCE all the kings save one were his kin, and an edict reserved kingdoms for the Liu. In the early Western Han the kingdoms covered two-thirds of the empire and their kings appointed most of their own officials. After the Rebellion of the Seven States in 154 BCE the court cut their powers: from 145 BCE they could appoint no official above 400 bushels and became nominal lords, living on a share of their kingdom’s taxes. Below them were the marquesses, the top two of the twenty ranks: a full marquess (liehou) drew part of the taxes of a fief the size of a county, an ordinary marquess only a pension. Kings’ sons, princesses’ husbands and high officials were made marquesses, and a marquess of a thousand households drew some 200,000 cash a year.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Kingdoms passed to the king’s eldest son by his queen, but from 127 BCE all a king’s sons were made marquesses with fiefs from his lands, so kingdoms shrank with each generation. A king who rebelled, or even murdered a slave, could lose his kingdom.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count of the nobles and their households; there were twenty kingdoms in 2 CE.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-xin">Han Xin</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ying-bu">Ying Bu</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-he" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/xiao-he.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Officials and scholars <span class="soc-term">guan, shi</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Many Han officials studied the books of Confucius and were chosen for being good sons and honest men.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Those who served in government held a privileged place just below the nobles. An official could not be arrested without the emperor’s leave, and one condemned to death was often allowed to take his own life instead. Officials were graded by salary in bushels of grain, about half paid in coin: the three highest ministers drew 10,000 bushels, the Nine Ministers 2,000 and the lowest clerks 100. Men entered office by recommendation. From 134 BCE, on Dong Zhongshu’s advice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> had every commandery send men judged “filial and incorrupt” (xiaolian) to the capital each year, and in 124 BCE he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-academy">Imperial Academy</a> (Taixue) to teach the Confucian classics; it had fifty students at first and some 30,000 by the 2nd century CE. Office was open in principle to educated commoners other than merchants, but in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a> the same families filled the high posts: a third of the officials with biographies in the Book of Later Han were sons or grandsons of officials.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A young man rose by study, a patron’s recommendation or service as a clerk in his commandery. Wealth could also buy office when the court sold posts, as it did on a large scale under Emperor Ling (168–189).</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count; the Imperial Academy alone had some 30,000 students by the 2nd century CE.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-he">Xiao He</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-academy">Imperial Academy</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">Scholar-officials</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-4"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yuan-shao.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Great landed families <span class="soc-term">haozu</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Rich Han families owned large farms, and poorer families rented their fields and gave them about half the harvest.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Below the officials, and often overlapping with them, stood the great landed families. Land could be bought and sold, and rich families, many founded by officials or merchants, built up large estates worked by tenants who paid about half their harvest as rent, along with hired labourers and a few slaves. They kept retainers, guests who lived on the estate and served as advisers, bodyguards or fighters. The Western Han court feared them: from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>’s reign thousands of noble and wealthy families were moved by force to the region of the capital, and attempts to limit landholding under Emperor Ai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a> failed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a>, restored with the help of great landholding families, relied on them, and their sons filled the offices: for 46 of the 110 years from 86 to 196 CE a member of the Yuan or Yang clan held one of the three highest posts. When the state weakened, landless peasants became their tenants and retainers.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Families rose through office or trade and bought land with the profits. The court could resettle them or confiscate their estates, but by the late Western Han, Cho-yun Hsu argues, moving them by force had become unthinkable.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">5</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>Most people (the rest)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Farmers <span class="soc-term">nong</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Most people in Han China were farmers, and men had to work for the government for one month each year.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Farmers were the great majority and, in Confucian eyes, the “root” of the state, second only to scholars. In the Western Han most were small owner-cultivators, ranked above tenants and hired labourers: by the official Chao Cuo’s reckoning a family of five farmed no more than about 100 mu, some 11 acres. The land tax was light, a thirtieth of the crop for most of the dynasty, but adults paid a poll tax of 120 coins a year, and men aged fifteen to fifty-six owed a month’s labour each year on public works. Until conscription was largely abandoned after 30 CE, each man also trained a year as a soldier and served a year on the frontier or in the capital. Debt, floods and taxes drove many to sell their land, even their children, and become tenants paying half the crop; by the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a> most peasants were landless. Their distress fed risings such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-eyebrows">Red Eyebrows</a> (about 18 CE) and the Yellow Turbans (184).</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Any free man could earn ranks of honour, granted on great occasions or bought with grain, that brought a better seat at village feasts, lighter punishments and relief from labour service. Study could carry a farmer’s son into office.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other tiers: the great majority, most of them farming families. This count also takes in the nobles, officials, great families, artisans and merchants, whose numbers are unknown.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">Corvée labour</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-eyebrows">Red Eyebrows</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-turban-rebellion">Yellow Turban Rebellion</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-6"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">6</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Artisans <span class="soc-term">gong</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Han craftspeople made shiny lacquer cups, bronze mirrors and fine silk cloth.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Artisans stood between farmers and merchants in the Confucian order. Scholars thought their work less essential than farming, since the state lived on grain, but did not despise it, and the law treated artisans better than merchants: they could wear <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, ride horses and carriages, and hold office. Some worked for themselves or for great households; others worked in government workshops, where master craftsmen were well paid to make bronze mirrors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">lacquerware</a>, silks and weapons, while convicts, conscripted labourers and state slaves did the heavy work. Han craftsmen were highly skilled: lacquered cups and boxes went out as luxury goods, a pair of metal calipers bears the very day it was made, and in 105 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-lun">Cai Lun</a>, director of the imperial workshops, presented an improved way of making paper. Skills were handed down by word of mouth from father to son.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Skill could make an artisan prosperous, and no law kept him from office, though few took that path: one painter who worked at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-academy">Imperial Academy</a> turned down repeated nominations.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">Lacquerware</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">7</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Merchants <span class="soc-term">shang</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Some Han merchants grew very rich, but laws kept many of them from wearing silk or working for the government.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Merchants came last of the four occupations, scorned by scholars as parasites, yet many were rich. Registered merchants, mostly small shopkeepers of the city markets, paid commercial taxes and a double poll tax of 240 coins, had to wear white, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>’s reign were barred from wearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, riding horses and holding office. They could be singled out for conscription and sent to settle the malarial south, and in 119 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> forbade them to buy land. Great traders who moved goods between cities often escaped registration, and a fortune of ten million cash, fifty times a marquess’s yearly income, marked a great merchant. To break the salt and iron magnates, some of whom employed a thousand peasants or more, Emperor Wu nationalised both industries by 117 BCE and put former merchants such as Sang Hongyang in charge. Trade along the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> carried Chinese silk to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Merchants bought land with their profits despite the bans, and a few entered office when the court needed their skills, as under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>, though an edict of 7 BCE again barred them from the bureaucracy.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-and-iron-monopoly">Salt and iron monopoly</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-8"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">8</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>0.5–1.5%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Enslaved people <span class="soc-term">nubi</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; in Han China there were few of them.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Slaves (nubi) were few, perhaps one person in a hundred, far fewer than in the Roman world. Some belonged to the state: prisoners of war, the families of men convicted of treason, gifts from foreign rulers and slaves confiscated from their owners, put to work in palaces, offices, workshops and stables. Others were privately owned, often peasants who had sold themselves for debt; they cooked, served, guarded their masters or performed as musicians, dancers and acrobats, and fewer worked the fields, where tenants were probably cheaper. Owners paid a poll tax of 240 coins a year on each slave, and slaves’ children were born slaves. Masters could beat their slaves but not kill them: kings lost their kingdoms for murdering slaves. The emperor could free state slaves for old age or merit, and private slaves could buy their freedom. In 9 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a> banned the buying and selling of slaves, but the ban was lifted in 12.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Freedom came by the emperor’s grant, a master’s choice or purchase. In one exceptional case the former slave Jin Midi rose to be one of the regents of the empire.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Roughly 1 per cent of the population, the figure given by Michael Loewe and A. F. P. Hulsewé.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a></div></div></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="han-china">Be born in Han China</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>Han society was more open than the four occupations suggest, above all in the Western Han, when, Patricia Ebrey notes, office and promotion were open to a wider part of the people than later. The founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> was a commoner, and Gongsun Hong, a former pig-breeder, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>’s chancellor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-qing">Wei Qing</a>, the son of a servant woman in a princess’s household, worked as a sheepherder and stableboy before his half-sister Wei Zifu became Emperor Wu’s empress; he rose to be Grand General and led the armies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. Recommendation as filial and incorrupt and study at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-academy">Imperial Academy</a> carried educated commoners into office, ranks of honour rewarded ordinary men, and merchants turned their fortunes into land. Falls were as common: debt turned owner-farmers into tenants or slaves, and the emperor could strip a noble of his fief or an official of his life. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a> great families had closed ranks, and the same clans filled the high offices.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>A Han woman was expected to obey her father, then her husband, then her adult son; the historian Ban Zhao wrote in her Lessons for Women that a woman’s great virtues were respect and compliance. Practice was looser. Mothers outranked their sons, an empress could give orders to her own father, and an empress dowager could rule for a child emperor and choose his successor. Sons divided the family property equally, while daughters received dowries, usually smaller but sometimes as large as a brother’s share. Women could sometimes initiate divorce, and remarriage, though frowned upon, was common, and a law of 189 BCE raised the poll tax fivefold on unmarried women aged fifteen to thirty. Women were exempt from labour service but worked hard: they raised children and wove cloth for the family and the market, sometimes in spinning groups or textile businesses employing hundreds of women, and some became physicians, merchants, singers or sorceresses.</p></section>
